The reason for a resistance network is to give ordinary people collective bargaining power over the things companies need from them—attention, data, content, and purchases.

People depend on companies for their livelihood. If AI reduces the demand for their work, their ability to negotiate through employment could weaken further. Yet those same companies still need people to watch, contribute, trust their services, and buy their products. The network would let people organize around that dependence.

Individually, you can ask a company to pay for your attention or respect your data preferences. The company can ignore your request. When millions of people use apps that follow shared rules, companies have an economic reason to accept those terms.

The network would make four things possible.
- People set the terms for their attention. Companies make paid offers that people can accept, decline, or price themselves.
- People control their data and creations. A video app receives permission to distribute your video under an agreement. Additional uses require the permissions and payments you choose.
- People organize their purchasing power. Communities can bring their demand together and ask companies to compete for their business.
- People govern the infrastructure they depend on. Members control the registration authority and common rules. Independent apps compete within those rules, and members can move between them.
AI agents would participate through registered operators, with defined permissions and charges. That gives people a way to decide how automated systems interact with their resources.

“Resistance” means having the collective ability to refuse unfair terms and sustain an alternative. For that ability to last, members must also be able to challenge the network’s leadership, change its rules, and move their data and business elsewhere. Those powers are what make community ownership meaningful.

We need a Resistance Network so people can negotiate collectively over the value they bring to the digital economy.

People depend on companies for wages. If AI reduces the demand for their work, that dependence could leave them with even less bargaining power. Companies, however, would still need customers, audiences, creators, and information about what people want.

The network would help people organize those contributions and set their own terms.

Imagine a million people agreeing that any app serving them must respect a few common rules.
- My creations belong to me. I decide who can distribute my videos and what they pay.
- My data requires my permission. I choose its purpose, price, and permitted duration of use.
- My attention has a rate. Companies can offer to buy my time, and I can decline.
- My purchasing power can join with others. We can ask companies to compete for our collective business.
A community registration service, independent apps, and automatic small payments would make those choices usable in everyday life. A company could earn money by providing a service, with its fees and permissions agreed upfront.

The network would also give people somewhere to move their activity when they reject a company’s terms. The ability to refuse becomes economically meaningful when people have a usable alternative and can act together.

Its community ownership would need to extend to decisions about membership, fees, disputes, and changes to the rules. Members would have the power to hold the network itself accountable.
